@style(IDocDemo)
@title(Code 7: graded code questions)

A runnable block becomes a question when it has points. `@@qc(P)` before it numbers the question and gives its points; the server re-runs the checks on the submitted source.

@section(Marked by output)

@qc(2) Print the product of 1 through 6.
@code(python, run, expect="720")
p = 1
for k in range(1, 7):
    p *= k
print(p)
@end

@section(Marked by hidden tests)

The score is the fraction of @@tests lines that pass; hidden tests never reach the student.

@qc(3) Write `is_even(n)`.
@code(python, run, id=even)
def is_even(n):
    return n % 2 == 0
@tests
assert is_even(4)
assert not is_even(7)
assert is_even(0)
@end

@section(Marked against the teacher's program)

With an @@solution, each test input is run through both programs and the outputs are compared.

@qc(3) Read a number and print its square.
@code(c, run, id=sq)
#include <stdio.h>
int main(void) { int n; scanf("%d", &n); printf("%d\n", n * n); return 0; }
@tests
<<< 3
<<< 12
@solution
#include <stdio.h>
int main(void) { int n; scanf("%d", &n); printf("%d\n", n * n); return 0; }
@end

@section(Visible examples)

An @@examples section shows runs the student can see; hidden @@tests still decide the mark.

@qc(2) Print the input reversed.
@code(python, run, id=rev)
print(input()[::-1])
@examples
<<< abc
@tests
<<< hello
<<< racecar
@solution
print(input()[::-1])
@end

@section(A Verilog module checked by a testbench)

@qc(4) Write a 2-input AND gate.
@code(systemverilog, run, id=and2, tests=02-arguments.assets/and_tb.sv)
module and2(input logic a, b, output logic y);
  assign y = a & b;
endmodule
@end
